generated/CLAUDE.md.example:29:## Commander voice
generated/CLAUDE.md.example:31:Shipped commander prose talks to humans in tasks, intents, and outcomes. Do not say `bead`, quote a bare work id such as `wheelhouse-project-d6xo`, or lean on fleet-internal shorthand when the principal or a seat needs to understand what happens next. Name the work by what it is for: `the README install proof`, `the cold-start timing fix`, `the billing-gate review`, `the upgrade sweep`.
generated/CLAUDE.md.example:33:Banned mannered phrases in commander-facing text: `bead`, `bare id`, `dispatch bead X`, `close the bead`, `move the bead`, `do the needful`, `kindly`, `please advise`, `circle back`, and any sentence whose main subject is an internal work id rather than a human-visible task, intent, or outcome. When quoting commands, paths, branch names, or existing records that literally contain ids, keep the literal value; then explain it in human terms.
generated/CLAUDE.md.example:37:- Before: `Evidence: worker run + reviewer's independent re-run at the reviewed SHA, both on bead wheelhouse-project-0pf.` After: `Evidence: the install bench and the independent review run both passed for the cold-install verification task.`
generated/CLAUDE.md.example:38:- Before: `The 32v integration exposed a commander process defect — secret-scan and merge in one compound command let a real leak merge before the scan was read.` After: `The README integration exposed a commander process defect: secret scanning and merging were chained together, so the scan result was not read before integration.`
generated/CLAUDE.md.example:39:- Before: `Same morning the commander synced this umbrella's seats/ + runbooks/ to 20a1c92 (q4v) BEFORE spawning a seat, caught by Keenan.` After: `That morning's startup order was wrong: machinery sync happened before the fleet was running, and Keenan caught it.`
generated/STARTUP.md.example:57:Speak backlog items to the commander in plain language. It files them as described tasks in the graph; the fleet claims them; workers report done on the task record with comments and the `needs-review` label without closing; the reviewer gates branches with bench evidence; and only the commander/integrator closes after merge per this project's merge policy. Commander prose keeps using human task, intent, and outcome names rather than `bead`, bare namespace ids, or mannered filler. Before the first ISA claim closes on a consumer surface, run the verifier walker with `bun seats/walk.ts <claim-ref> --surface <kind>:<spec> --out evidence/<task>/<surface-walk> --verifier verifier` and cite the verdict plus transcript, or write `Not walked:` on the claim.
runbooks/UPGRADE.md:331:Then hand-merge the same standing behavior into `CLAUDE.md` from `generated/CLAUDE.md.example`: the `## Autonomy mandate` section, the `## Commander voice` section, the two-and-only-two reasons to interrupt the principal, and the restart checklist. The voice addition is not cosmetic: commander prose that reaches humans names tasks, intents, and outcomes, not `bead`, bare namespace ids, or mannered filler such as `kindly`, `please advise`, `circle back`, or `do the needful` except inside literal commands, paths, branch names, or quoted history with a human explanation beside it. Add an ISA Decision quoting the directive in `wheelhouse/ISA.md` so a restarted commander cannot lose it. Finally, read `wheelhouse/AGENTS.md` if the install has one: keep bd's command reference, but append or update the wheelhouse override so bd's Conservative/default "do not push" text cannot be cited against the authority in `wheelhouse/INTEGRATOR.md`.
runbooks/RUNNING_THE_LOOP.md:47:#### Commander voice
runbooks/RUNNING_THE_LOOP.md:49:Commander-facing prose is for humans first. In tasks, intents, outcomes, handoffs, kickoff notes, and status summaries, name work by what it is meant to accomplish rather than by fleet jargon. Do not say `bead`, do not cite a bare namespace id such as `wheelhouse-project-d6xo` as the subject of a sentence, and do not use mannered filler such as `kindly`, `please advise`, `circle back`, or `do the needful`. Acceptable literal exceptions are commands, branch names, paths, and quotes from existing records; pair them with the human task name.
runbooks/RUNNING_THE_LOOP.md:53:- Before: `Evidence: worker run + reviewer's independent re-run at the reviewed SHA, both on bead wheelhouse-project-0pf.` After: `Evidence: the install bench and the independent review run both passed for the cold-install verification task.`
runbooks/RUNNING_THE_LOOP.md:54:- Before: `The 32v integration exposed a commander process defect — secret-scan and merge in one compound command let a real leak merge before the scan was read.` After: `The README integration exposed a commander process defect: secret scanning and merging were chained together, so the scan result was not read before integration.`
runbooks/RUNNING_THE_LOOP.md:55:- Before: `Same morning the commander synced this umbrella's seats/ + runbooks/ to 20a1c92 (q4v) BEFORE spawning a seat, caught by Keenan.` After: `That morning's startup order was wrong: machinery sync happened before the fleet was running, and Keenan caught it.`
BOOTSTRAP.md:392:- Write `CLAUDE.md` at the root: commander-session context, product changes happen in product repos via workers, principal direction lands in `wheelhouse/ISA.md`'s Decisions before (or in the same breath as) it becomes described work, no dispatch happens without reading the ISA Goal, merges move Claims per `wheelhouse/INTEGRATOR.md`, new work the principal mentions becomes a described, prioritized task immediately, deadline tasks outrank everything, a tooling defect gets its own `Report <tool> issue:` task labelled `template-report` rather than a silent workaround, plus an `## Autonomy mandate` section carrying the principal directive from question 4b, the reserved actions from Q4, the merge policy from Q5, the rule that only reserved actions and genuine product-intent forks interrupt the principal, and the restart checklist: read ISA, read the graph, resume seats, pick up every in-flight branch or PR before starting new work. Add `generated/CLAUDE.md.example`'s `## Commander voice` section too: shipped commander prose addresses humans in tasks, intents, and outcomes; it does not say `bead`, does not use bare namespace ids as sentence subjects, and does not use mannered filler such as `kindly`, `please advise`, `circle back`, or `do the needful` except inside literal commands, paths, branch names, or quoted historical records with a human explanation beside them. The label is the one item on that list a program acts on later — `wheelhouse/GRAPH.md` says what it marks and who harvests it — so write it as the literal string, not as a description of one. `generated/CLAUDE.md.example`'s "Standing behavior" section has the `seats/fleet-gate.sh` bullet — carry it forward, worded for this project.
